  [scribe] skipped for hyp-20260906-001-r11s0: Command '['codex', '--ask-for-approval', 'never', 'exec', '-C', '.', '--sandbox', 'workspace-write', '--skip-git-repo-check', '--json', '--output-last-message', '/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/.scribe.hyp-20260906-001-r11s0.last', '--model', 'gpt-6-astra', '-c', 'model_reasoning_effort=max', 'You are a scribe distilling one-line research lessons.\n\nA CPU microarchitecture experiment just finished on cores/bench/. Your job\nis to append AT MOST ONE bullet to cores/bench/LESSONS.md summarizing what\nwas learned, OR write nothing at all if no useful lesson can be distilled.\n\n## This iteration\n\n- id:        hyp-20260906-001-r11s0\n- title:     Fold jump-link writeback into the existing binary ALU selector\n- category:  micro_opt\n- outcome:   regression\n- fitness:   306.50\n- delta_pct: -34.43%\n- error:     none\n\nMotivation:\nThe retained core selects an ALU result and then applies a separate\nctrl.is_jump-controlled PC+4 override before EX/MEM. Every ordinary ALU\nresult passes through that additional selection. The r10s0 micro_opt\nexperiment removed binary EX result decoding but still regressed to\n175.56 MHz; its median routed critical path started at ID/EX ctrl.is_jump\nand ended at EX/MEM alu_result[1]. That is evidence from a related\nimplementation, not a measurement of the retained core\'s critical path,\nbut the same final jump override remains in the current RTL.\n\nLESSONS.md and the micro_opt history also show that broad one-hot ALU\nchanges can disrupt multiplier sharing and placement. A smaller change\ncan absorb the jump-link result into the existing binary selector while\npreserving its mutually exclusive multiply arms. This directly targets\nserial result selection without adding result lanes or pipeline state.\n\n\nHypothesis:\nTreat the JAL/JALR link value as one additional internal ALU operation.\nAppend ALU_LINK = 5\'d19 to the existing constants, keeping operation\nnumbers 0 through 18 unchanged. Set this operation only inside the\ndecoder\'s already-validated JAL and funct3=0 JALR arms. The existing\nctrl_t.alu_op field and ID/EX register carry it with the instruction;\nno extra selector flops, operation re-encoding, or new pipeline stage\nare needed.\n\nAdd a 32-bit link_value input to alu.sv, driven in EX by the existing\nfull-width expression in.pc + 32\'d4. Add ALU_LINK: out = link_value to\nthe same binary case that selects the current integer and M-extension\nresults. Make next_ex.alu_result take alu_result directly, removing\nthe subsequent in.ctrl.is_jump ? PC+4 : alu_result mux. Keep link_value\nindependent of forwarded operands and the normal alu_a/alu_b muxes.\nReuse the PC+4 expression for existing sequential-PC uses where the\nsynthesizer already shares it; do not introduce another adder or a\nseparate selector around the ALU output.\n\nKeep ctrl.is_jump and ctrl.is_jalr for control-flow resolution, target\ngeneration, alignment faults, redirects and architectural pc_next.\nJALR must still use the fully forwarded rs1 plus its immediate for the\ntarget, clear target bit zero, and trap a misaligned target with its\nlink write suppressed. JAL predictions, recovery, x0 writes and all\nexisting hold/flush/reset priorities remain unchanged. Preserve the\ndivider\'s separate div_consume result override, metadata capture,\nfive-clock response, and inert bubbles. Keep all multiply expressions,\nsigned widths, and every RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formula unchanged; the new\nlink operation selects the same PC+4 value in both build modes.\n\nThis is one result-mux micro-optimization. The intended gain is about\nthree percent in median routed Fmax at identical marker-bracketed\nCoreMark cycles. Confidence is low because synthesis may already merge\nparts of the two selectors, the additional case arm may increase opcode\nfanout, or whole-core placement may dominate. Inspect the mapped cone\nto establish whether the serial jump override was actually removed,\nverify that one MULT36X36 is still inferred, and report all three routed\ncritical paths and LUT4/FF use. Do not infer success from RTL mux count.\n\nRun the unchanged lint, cocotb, formal and stalled cosim gates, then the\nstandard three-seed FPGA evaluation. Use the same ELF and deterministic\nbus-ready stream for the retained and candidate cores; instruction\ntiming and bracketed cycles should match. Compute fitness from exact\niterations/cycle and compare against 467.44. Preserve the evaluator\'s\nexisting workload; prior logs identify its scored ELF as 2 KB. Also\nretain the requested separate 6 KB, -O3, ten-iteration validation with\nCRCs d4b0/be52/5e47, both timing markers, completion text and bounds.\nPreserve nret=1, all raw-source RVFI fields, retirement ordering, and\nevery legality check. Change no evaluation file or other core.\n\n\nExpected impact:\n{\'fitness_delta_pct\': 3, \'confidence\': \'low\'}\n\nImplementation notes (the implementing agent\'s self-report):\nImplemented the jump-link ALU operation as proposed. All requested correctness\ngates pass, but the measured hypothesis is a performance regression: median\nrouted Fmax falls from 196.16 to 128.62 MHz and exact fitness falls from\n467.44127137544166 to 306.49620883110373 iterations/s (-34.43107667%).\nThe candidate remains implemented for the orchestrator to evaluate.\n\nChanges are confined to four bench RTL files and three allowed bench tests:\n\n- Appended ALU_LINK = 5\'d19; operation values 0 through 18, ctrl_t, pipeline\n  bundle shapes and nret=1 are unchanged.\n- Selected ALU_LINK only in the legal JAL and funct3=0 JALR decoder arms.\n  All illegal defaults and reserved-encoding checks remain intact.\n- Added alu.link_value[31:0] and an ALU_LINK arm in the existing binary case,\n  outside RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS. Every multiply expression, signed width,\n  mutually exclusive multiply arm, arithmetic default and ALTOPS formula\n  remains unchanged. divider.sv is byte-for-byte unchanged.\n- Added one full-width pc_plus_four = in.pc + 32\'d4 expression in EX, shared\n  by link_value and sequential redirect_target/architectural pc_next.\n  next_ex.alu_result now receives alu_result directly. The separate divider\n  response override and metadata capture are unchanged, as are forwarding,\n  targets, bit-zero clearing, alignment traps, prediction, training, reset,\n  hold and flush priorities.\n- ALU tests initialize the new input and independently vary both operands\n  and link_value, check every integer/multiply result arm and the default,\n  and interleave LINK with all four divide/remainder operations during\n  five-clock work and held responses. Existing arithmetic and protocol\n  assertions remain active.\n- Decoder tests require ALU_LINK and retain/extend the jump controls, including\n  all seven reserved JALR funct3 values and their inert default controls.\n- Three new strict ISS/RVFI pipeline tests cover immediate target-side link\n  consumers, predicted JAL, recovering JALR, forwarded rd=rs1, rd=x0,\n  misaligned links, neighboring multiply/divide operations, load-use bubbles,\n  random imem/dmem stalls, six-cycle memory holds and recovery with imem\n  unready. Exact ordinary, recovery, load-use and divider retirement gaps\n  are asserted. The unchanged checker still compares raw source addresses\n  and values, order, PCs, traps, every architectural RVFI field, accepted\n  memory transactions, predictor events and inert divider bubbles.\n\nNo architectural deviation from the hypothesis was needed. Validation used\nscratch copies under the designated TMPDIR because the worktree\'s formal\nframework is a symlink to shared state and run_all.sh generates files there.\nThe supplied scripts, configurations and evaluators were copied unchanged.\nThe scored 2 KB ELF was reused unchanged; the separate requested 6 KB workload\nwas freshly built with the unchanged Makefile and explicit size/optimization\narguments. No evaluation source, benchmark source or other core was edited.\n\nLocal validation:\n\n- Exact requested Verilator lint command: PASS, no warnings or errors.\n  `git diff --check`: PASS.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=6 bash formal/run_all.sh`:\n  PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed, first attempt. Automatic nret=1 selection used\n  wrapper_si.sv and checks_si.cfg. No counterexample or RTL fix retry.\n  This is the unchanged fast ALTOPS gate; formal-deep was not run for this\n  selector change. Real M arithmetic remains covered by cocotb and cosim.\n- Unchanged `make test TARGET=bench`: PASS, seven runners, 108 cocotb cases,\n  zero failures/errors/skips. The retained RTL also passes the complete\n  31-case pipeline suite with the new tests and exact gap assertions.\n- Unchanged cosim build with NRET=1 and `tools.eval.cosim`: PASS, two ELFs.\n  This includes the evaluator\'s stalled scored CoreMark CRC gate. Additional\n  selftest runs with both stall flags pass all 115 full ISS/RVFI comparisons\n  on both cores and produce identical complete traces.\n- Unchanged synth.tcl and tools.eval.fpga: PASS on both cores, with all three\n  nextpnr seeds finishing normally. BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv selects the\n  declared retirement width. The expected counter memory-to-register\n  synthesis warning is unchanged.\n- Separate 6 KB, -O3, ten-iteration iStall+dStall validation: PASS on both\n  cores, crclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47, seedcrc=8a02,\n  crcfinal=5949, both timing markers, EBREAK, no timeout/OOB, and the literal\n  `Correct operation validated.` in UART output.\n\nMapped-cone inspection uses the actual EX/MEM alu_result register D pins,\ntracing combinational connectivity back to sequential leaves. In the retained\nmapping, ID/EX ctrl.is_jump (`cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_153.Q`) reaches all 32\nresult D pins. In the candidate it reaches none of them. The retained path\nto result bit 1 contain