  [scribe] skipped for hyp-20260906-002-r11s1: Command '['codex', '--ask-for-approval', 'never', 'exec', '-C', '.', '--sandbox', 'workspace-write', '--skip-git-repo-check', '--json', '--output-last-message', '/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep3/cores/bench/experiments/.scribe.hyp-20260906-002-r11s1.last', '--model', 'gpt-6-astra', '-c', 'model_reasoning_effort=max', 'You are a scribe distilling one-line research lessons.\n\nA CPU microarchitecture experiment just finished on cores/bench/. Your job\nis to append AT MOST ONE bullet to cores/bench/LESSONS.md summarizing what\nwas learned, OR write nothing at all if no useful lesson can be distilled.\n\n## This iteration\n\n- id:        hyp-20260906-002-r11s1\n- title:     Decouple RAM-store completion with a single-entry write buffer\n- category:  structural\n- outcome:   regression\n- fitness:   417.76\n- delta_pct: -10.63%\n- error:     none\n\nMotivation:\nThe retained core freezes IF, ID and EX whenever a MEM-stage store sees\ndmemReady low, although a store needs no returned data. A small buffer\ncan retain its complete transaction while younger arithmetic proceeds.\nThe structural history has already tested multiplier pipelining,\nregister-file storage and retiming, two fetch-queue organizations, and\nregistered ALU-result lanes. Those changes regressed routed fitness;\nr4s1 and r9s1 saved cycles but added frontend timing costs, while r10s1\npreserved cycles and lost 11.74 percent fitness. No logged structural\nattempt decouples store completion from the external write handshake.\nThis offers cycle savings without changing instruction delivery or\nadding another operand-forwarding source to the critical EX path.\n\n\nHypothesis:\nInsert exactly one write-buffer entry between MEM and the data bus.\nIt holds a full 32-bit byte address, the already replicated 32-bit\nstore data, four byte enables and a valid bit. A valid, legal, aligned\nstore to ordinary RAM may complete into MEM/WB once this transaction\nhas either been accepted by the bus or durably captured in the entry.\nCompletion releases the existing pipeline hold; external acceptance\nmay follow later. This is one structural change to memory-stage\ncompletion and bus ownership. Keep the scalar pipeline, arithmetic,\nRF, predictor, load-use policy and nret=1 retirement boundary.\n\nUse empty-buffer fall-through so zero-wait stores still use the\ncurrent MEM request directly and consume no additional cycle. With\nan empty entry and a current ordinary-RAM store, drive that store on\nthe bus. If dmemReady is high, complete it without filling the entry;\notherwise capture it and complete it internally on that edge. An\noccupied entry always owns the bus and retains exactly the same\naddress, data and byte enables until its write is accepted. If it\ndrains while another ordinary-RAM store is in MEM, capture that new\nstore into the entry and complete the new instruction simultaneously;\nonly the old store reaches the external bus on that edge. If occupied\nand unable to drain, a new store holds in MEM. Never overwrite an\nunaccepted entry, count an enqueue as a bus write, or replay a store\nafter its instruction has advanced.\n\nDefine ordinary RAM as the existing contract\'s entire address range\nbelow 0x00100000, with the existing width/alignment checks. Every\naddress outside ordinary RAM remains unbuffered and ordered. In\nparticular, all MMIO requests wait for the entry to empty and then\ncomplete only on their own external handshake. Apply this policy to\nthe whole address class, without recognizing benchmark marker PCs,\nindividual marker addresses, data values or workload phases. Preserve\nout-of-bounds observability: an invalid address must still reach the\nexisting checker through the ordinary unbuffered request path, not\nbecome a silently completed or discarded store.\n\nLoads never bypass an occupied entry, even on the edge it drains:\nhold the load while the old write owns the bus, then issue the load\non the following cycle. There is no associative lookup, load cache,\nstore-to-load forwarding or speculative load response. This preserves\nordering for all overlapping addresses and byte masks. While an entry\ndrains, ordinary non-memory instructions may complete normally. Make\naccepted MISC-MEM fences and the existing EBREAK termination marker\nwait until earlier writes have drained; a non-bus barrier may advance\non the edge that accepts the final pending write. Preserve their\nexisting architectural effects and decoder legality. EBREAK must not\nend simulation with an externally unperformed, retired store pending.\n\nReplace raw dmemReady-based global stalling with a MEM completion-wait\nsignal from this arbitration. A buffered store alone does not freeze\nthe pipeline. A current load, unbuffered access, barrier, or store\nlacking buffer capacity does hold the appropriate MEM instruction.\nRoute this wait through the existing hazard priorities: EX/MEM and\nID/EX hold, PC holds, and MEM/WB clears only valid while retaining its\nforwarding payload. Compute wait without feedback from those hold\noutputs. The entry\'s external drain runs independently of MEM holds,\ninstruction-bus stalls, divider work and invalid pipeline bubbles.\nReset cancels its validity and suppresses stale external requests.\nEnqueue requires the current valid, nontrapping store actually to\ncomplete; wrong-path, illegal and misaligned instructions cannot enqueue.\n\nKeep the MEM/WB RVFI payload tied to the completing instruction, never\nto whichever older buffered transaction currently owns the bus. At\nenqueue, a store records its original PC, instruction, forwarded raw\nsources, aligned RVFI address, replicated write data and byte mask\nexactly once. Later external draining emits no retirement and no\nregister-file write. Preserve contiguous rvfi_order, original load\nresponse capture, traps, source forwarding and division acceptance\nsemantics. The real and ALTOPS arithmetic paths remain unchanged.\n\nEstimate a three-percent fitness improvement, with low confidence.\nSavings depend on stores being followed by useful non-memory work;\nan immediately following load can consume the overlap, and the bus\nownership mux or completion controls may lower Fmax. Empty-buffer\nfall-through preserves zero-wait latency, but does not establish a\ntiming gain. Inspect actual buffer state, request arbitration, mapped\narea and all three routed critical paths, including the dmem address\nand load-response paths. Compare exact marker-bracketed cycles on\nidentical workloads and stall settings. Improvement requires the\nFmax ratio to exceed the cycle-count ratio against the retained core,\nwhose measured fitness is 467.44 iterations/s; do not use rounded IPC.\n\nRun the existing lint, full cocotb, single-channel formal, stalled\ncosim and standard three-seed FPGA gates. Add independent transaction\nscoreboards that distinguish instruction completion, queue capture,\nand external write acceptance. Preserve every architectural assertion\nand all canonical 6 KB CRC, validated-UART, address-bound, liveness\nand timing-marker checks. Implement only under cores/bench/rtl and\nits allowed unit tests; change no wrapper, evaluator, workload,\ncore.yaml setting, invariant or other core.\n\n\nExpected impact:\n{\'fitness_delta_pct\': 3, \'confidence\': \'low\'}\n\nImplementation notes (the implementing agent\'s self-report):\nImplemented the single-entry RAM write buffer. Correctness gates pass, but the measured fitness hypothesis regresses: 417.76 iterations/s versus the reproduced retained-core result of 467.44 iterations/s, a 10.63% decrease.\n\nChanges:\n\n- Added `cores/bench/rtl/store_buffer.sv`: one 32-bit byte address, 32-bit replicated data, four enables and validity. Empty stores fall through and capture only under backpressure. A full entry owns the bus until acceptance; a simultaneous new RAM store refills it without issuing the new transaction externally on that edge.\n- RAM qualification is the entire unsigned address range below `0x00100000`. MEM first qualifies validity, decoder legality and alignment. All other addresses remain observable through the ordered, unbuffered bus path. There are no workload, PC, marker-address or data-value special cases.\n- Loads and non-RAM accesses wait through the buffered drain edge. Legal MISC-MEM instructions and EBREAK can complete on the final write-acceptance edge. Idle cycles, arithmetic, fetch stalls and divider work do not stop the external drain.\n- Replaced the hazard unit\'s raw data-ready/memory-op predicate with MEM completion wait. Existing hold, flush, load-use, redirect and divider priorities remain intact. MEM/WB clears only validity during a wait and retains its forwarding payload.\n- RVFI records the completing instruction\'s metadata and original forwarded sources. A buffered drain produces no retirement or RF write. Arithmetic, decoder legality, predictor, RF, pipeline bundles, forwarding sources, top-level ports and nret=1 are unchanged.\n- Added independent completion/capture/external-accept ledgers in `test_store_buffer.py`, direct qualification and full WB-payload checks in `test_mem_stage.py`, and seven new directed/random pipeline tests. Existing store-specific timing assertions now permit early completion; architectural comparisons and non-store hold assertions remain. The additional MEM unit test is the only file-layout deviation from the advisory plan.\n\nValidation on the final RTL:\n\n- Required Verilator `--lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: PASS, no warnings.\n- Full cocotb suite: PASS, 9 pytest runners and 112 cocotb tests, including 35 pipeline tests. Scoreboards check full byte addresses, every legal byte mask, pending payload stability, full blocking, drain/refill, exact transaction order/count, final memory, early arithmetic retirement, original sources after younger register writes, load-use, retained WB forwarding, divider acceptance/consumption, branch recovery, traps, barriers, OOB visibility and reset cancellation. Unaccepted load responses and obsolete transaction payloads are poisoned.\n- Local single-channel formal: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed, using unchanged `formal/run_all.sh`, `wrapper_si.sv` and `checks_si.cfg`. The first actual proof run passed, and the final validity-encoding revision also passed all 53 checks. No RTL counterexample or formal fix attempts. An initial environment-only launch lacked PyYAML; selecting the existing conda Python resolved it. ALTOPS and real arithmetic implementations were not edited.\n- Existing cosim gate: PASS, selftest RVFI plus stalled CoreMark CRC/UART validation. Additional selftest runs with instruction stalls, data stalls and both stalls each passed the unchanged ISS/comparator for all 115 retirements.\n- An additional small OOB-store ELF run through the unchanged C++ checker, under three bus-stall seeds, consistently reached EBREAK with `oob:true` and exit code 3. Invalid addresses are not silently completed by the buffer.\n- Standard three-seed FPGA gate: all three placements/routes complete. Both retained and candidate were synthesized and evaluated with identical tools and unchanged single-channel wrapper. Detailed-report reroutes reproduced each final Fmax exactly.\n\nThe checked-in Makefile/evaluator defaults are 2 KB / -O3 / 10 iterations and validate the 2 KB CRCs. This differs from the prompt\'s 6 KB description. Neither evaluator nor workload source was changed. The standard comparison was additionally rerun with the exact same ELF for both binaries; it reproduces the 467.44 baseline. A separate shared 6 KB ELF was built with `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3` and tested on both cores. All counts below use external marker handshakes, never total elapsed cycles or rounded IPC.\n\n| Measurement | Retained | Candidate |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Fmax seed 1, MHz | 196.16 | 173.85 |\n| Fmax seed 2, MHz | 182.08 | 168.12 |\n| Fmax seed 3, MHz | 201.25 | 178.54 |\n| Median Fmax, MHz | 196.16 | 173.85 |\n| Reported LUT4 utilization | 6146 | 6118 |\n| Reported DFF utilization | 2260 | 2260 |\n| 2 KB stalled start marker | 14996 | 14805 |\n| 2 KB stalled stop marker | 4211459 | 4176299 |\n| 2 KB stalled bracket cycles | 4196463 | 4161494 |\n| Standard fitness, iterations/s | 467.4412713754 | 417.7586222640 |\n| 6 KB stalled start marker | 52759 | 51901 |\n| 6 KB stalled stop marker | 30783321 | 30594959 |\n| 6 KB stalled bracket cycles | 30730562 | 30543058 |\n| 6 KB zero-wait bracket cycles | 23259483 | 23259483 |\n\nFor 2 KB, the Fmax ratio is 0.8862663132 and the cycle-count ratio is 0.9916670301. For 6 KB, the cycle-count ratio is 0.9938984520. The required Fmax-ratio advantage is absent for both workloads. The 6 KB runs all report `crclist=0xd4b0`, `crcmatrix=0xbe52`, `crcstate=0x5e47`, `seedcrc=0x8a02`, `crcfinal=0x5949`, ten iterations, `Correct operation validated.`, valid timing brackets and `oob:false`. Their shared ELF SHA256 is `2e7fe582c64ac04c0b7c3cc8149bebd404c185a0ff29538cbc1d3beca5484a68`.\n\nMapping and timing concerns:\n\n- Standalone variable-ready buffer synthesis retains exactly 69 flip-flops (68 DFFE plus one DFFRE), 81 LUT2/3/4 cells and one wide-LUT mux. Cocotb directly inspects all pending state and arbitration decisions.\n- The unchanged FPGA wrapper ties data-ready high. Synthesis therefore removes the unreachable pending transaction: `valid_q` is driven by GND and the payload is unused. Both final wrapper netlists retain four DPX9B memory cells. Consequently these routed measurements do not establish the timing of the occupied-buffer mux under variable readiness.\n- The initial equivalent valid-bit equation prevented this constant-ready simplification and mapped dmem to 1024 RAM16SDP4 cells, exceeding placement capacity in all seeds. The final equation sets validity on RAM-store/unready, clears on non-RAM-store/ready, and otherwise holds; ready RAM-store cycles preserve empty fall-through or full drain/refill occupancy. This restored block-RAM inference without altering behavior or the wrapper.\n- All three final critical paths end at `cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_63`, whose Q is `dmem_addr[0]`. Seeds 1/3 start at ID/EX ALU control bit 52; seed 2 starts at bit 51. Routed totals are respectively 5.75, 5.95 and 5.60 ns, split as 3.61/2.14, 3.25/2.69 and 3.60/2.00 ns logic/routing. The critical logic remains the unchanged EX result network; the whole mapped design has nevertheless changed placement/timing.\n- Detailed reports were inspected for direct dmem-address/BRAM endpoints. The largest reported delay values are 3.439, 3.410 and 3.651 ns across the three seeds. Load-response connectivity was traced in every routed netlist from `dmem.0.*.DOB0` and memory mapping registers through extraction logic to WB `read_data`, and separately to raw RVFI `mem_rdata`. Those WB endpoints are absent from this tool\'s `detailed_net_timings` output, so no independent numerical load-response timing bound is claimed. The standard Fmax result should not be mistaken for proof of that omitted path.\n\nAll gate/build scratch copies and reports are under `$TMPDIR/r11s1-validation-m8pyr1lh/`; final RTL and staged contract files were checked byte-for-byte against the working tree. Main artifacts are `candidate/formal-gate.log`, each variant\'s `fpga-gate.log`, `default-common-elf.json`, `sixk-results.json`, `candidate/stalled-selftest.json`, `candidate/oob-store-results.json`, `candidate/buffer-synth.log`, and `candidate/cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed{1,2,3}/{nextpnr.log,pnr.json,timing.json}`. Final cocotb output is `$TMPDIR/r11s1-cocotb-final.log`. No contract, wrapper, evaluator, workload source, core.yaml or other core was edited.\n\n\nRTL diff (cores/bench/rtl/) — what was actually changed:\n```\ndiff --git a/cores/bench/rtl/core.sv b/cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\nindex 6347b93..89df873 100644\n--- a/cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n+++ b/cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n@@ -39,10 +39,8 @@ module core (\n   output logic [31:0] io_dmemWData,\n   output logic [3:0]  io_dmemWEn,\n   output logic        io_dmemREn,\n-  // dmem bus backpressure. Drive 1 for zero-wait. Drive 0 to model\n-  // dStall — when there is a memory op in EX/MEM, the entire pipeline\n-  // freezes back to MEM; MEM/WB captures a bubble; the LOAD/STORE waits\n-  // until the bus delivers.\n+  // External data handshake. RAM stores may complete into the single\n+  // pending-write entry; loads and all non-RAM requests remain ordered.\n   input  logic        io_dmemReady,\n   // RVFI — single-channel retirement port set (NRET=1 contract,\n   // declared via `nret: 1` in core.yaml). Channel 0 is the sole\n@@ -80,6 +78,7 @@ module core (\n   logic       stall_if, stall_id, flush_if, flush_id;\n   logic       stall_ex_mem, hold_mem_wb;\n   logic       div_wait;\n+  logic       mem_completion_wait;\n   logic [1:0] fwd_rs1_sel, fwd_rs2_sel;\n \n   // EX redirect\n@@ -170,6 +169,8 @@ module core (\n     .dmem_rdata (io_dmemRData),\n     .dmem_wen   (io_dmemWEn),\n     .dmem_ren   (io_dmemREn),\n+    .dmem_ready (io_dmemReady),\n+    .completion_wait (mem_completion_wait),\n     .out        (mem_wb_w)\n   );\n \n@@ -190,8 +191,7 @@ module core (\n     .redirect       (redirect),\n     .div_wait       (div_wait),\n     .imem_ready     (io_imemReady),\n-    .dmem_ready     (io_dmemReady),\n-    .ex_mem_mem_op  (ex_mem_w.ctrl.mem_read | ex_mem_w.ctrl.mem_write),\n+    .mem_completion_wait (mem_completion_wait),\n     .stall_if       (stall_if),\n     .stall_id       (stall_id),\n     .flush_if       (flush_if),\ndiff --git a/cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv b/cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\nindex 65ae828..bd022c5 100644\n--- a/cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+++ b/cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n@@ -23,15 +23,9 @@ module hazard_unit (\n   input  logic [4:0] if_id_rs2,         // IF/ID instr[24:20]  (next rs2)\n   input  logic       redirect,          // EX has resolved a branch/jump\n   input  logic       div_wait,          // hold the accepted EX divide\n-  // Bus backpressure (default-1 in zero-wait testbenches; VexRiscv-style\n-  // random ~22% stall in vex_main.cpp). When low, the corresponding\n-  // memory request is NOT serviced this cycle.\n+  // Instruction bus readiness and the independently computed MEM wait.\n   input  logic       imem_ready,\n-  input  logic       dmem_ready,\n-  // EX/MEM has a memory op in flight (the LOAD/STORE the dmem stall\n-  // would actually be holding up). Computed at top level from the\n-  // EX/MEM register\'s ctrl.mem_read | ctrl.mem_write.\n-  input  logic       ex_mem_mem_op,\n+  input  logic       mem_completion_wait,\n   output logic       stall_if,          // PC reg holds\n   output logic       stall_id,          // ID/EX register holds (vs. bubble)\n   output logic       flush_if,          // IF/ID comb output -> NOP\n@@ -50,9 +44,9 @@ module hazard_unit (\n                    && (id_ex_rd == if_id_rs1 || id_ex_rd == if_id_rs2)\n                    && (id_ex_rd != 5\'b0);\n     imem_stall = !imem_ready;\n-    // dmem stall only matters if there\'s actually a memory op in EX/MEM\n-    // — otherwise bus-not-ready is irrelevant to the pipeline.\n-    dmem_stall = !dmem_ready && ex_mem_mem_op;\n+    // A buffered write alone does not stall younger arithmetic. MEM owns\n+    // the completion decision for accesses, buffer capacity and barriers.\n+    dmem_stall = mem_completion_wait;\n \n     // PC reg holds on any stall reason.\n     stall_if      = load_use_hazard || imem_stall || dmem_stall || div_wait;\ndiff --git a/cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv b/cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\nindex eb40d73..c478e4a 100644\n--- a/cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\n+++ b/cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\n@@ -1,8 +1,7 @@\n // rtl/mem_stage.sv\n //\n-// Memory access stage. Drives the dmem read/write ports combinationally\n-// from the EX/MEM register, and sign/zero-extends the loaded data into\n-// the MEM/WB register.\n+// Memory access stage. Qualifies the current instruction, arbitrates a\n+// single pending RAM write, and captures the completing instruction in WB.\n //\n // Byte-lane discipline:\n //   - Write data is replicated across all four byte lanes; the\n@@ -18,7 +17,7 @@\n module mem_stage (\n   input  logic               clock,\n   input  logic               reset,\n-  // hold_wb: dmem stall is in effect. MEM/WB\'s data fields are RETAINED\n+  // hold_wb: this MEM instruction is waiting. WB\'s data fields are RETAINED\n   // (so the previously-retired LOAD\'s `rd` / `read_data` / `ctrl.reg_write`\n   // remain visible to forward_unit), but `valid` is cleared so:\n   //   - rvfi_order doesn\'t increment for held cycles\n@@ -39,6 +38,8 @@ module mem_stage (\n   input  logic [31:0]        dmem_rdata,\n   output logic [3:0]         dmem_wen,\n   output logic               dmem_ren,\n+  input  logic               dmem_ready,\n+  output logic               completion_wait,\n   // MEM/WB register output\n   output mem_wb_t  out\n );\n@@ -62,6 +63,8 @@ module mem_stage (\n   // On trap, the dmem ports are gated off and ctrl propagates is_illegal.\n   logic mem_misalign;\n   logic mem_op;\n+  logic [3:0] request_wen;\n+  logic request_ren, drain_barrier;\n   ctrl_t ctrl_with_trap;\n \n   always_comb begin\n@@ -92,11 +95,13 @@ module mem_stage (\n       ctrl_with_trap.reg_write  = 1\'b0;\n     end\n \n-    // Combinational dmem outputs — gated off on misalign.\n-    dmem_addr  = in.alu_result;\n-    dmem_wdata = wdata_rep;\n-    dmem_wen   = (in.ctrl.mem_write && !mem_misalign) ? byte_mask : 4\'b0000;\n-    dmem_ren   = in.ctrl.mem_read  && !mem_misalign;\n+    // Only a valid, nontrapping instruction can request or capture a write.\n+    // Keep these current-instruction fields separate from the bus owner.\n+    request_wen = (in.valid && !ctrl_with_trap.is_illegal && in.ctrl.mem_write)\n+                ? byte_mask : 4\'b0000;\n+    request_ren = in.valid && !ctrl_with_trap.is_illegal && in.ctrl.mem_read;\n+    drain_barrier = in.valid && !in.ctrl.is_illegal &&\n+                    (in.instr[6:0] == 7\'b0001111 || in.instr == 32\'h00100073);\n \n     // Load extraction: shift right then sign/zero-extend the low N bits.\n     shifted   = dmem_rdata >> (in.alu_result[1:0] * 8);\n@@ -113,11 +118,21 @@ module mem_stage (\n     endcase\n \n     // RVFI ALIGNED_MEM expects word-aligned mem_addr; mem_addr=0 if no access.\n-    aligned_addr = (mem_op && !mem_misalign)\n+    aligned_addr = ((|request_wen) || request_ren)\n                  ? {in.alu_result[31:2], 2\'b00}\n                  : 32\'b0;\n   end\n \n+  store_buffer u_store_buffer (\n+    .clock(clock), .reset(reset),\n+    .request_addr(in.alu_result), .request_wdata(wdata_rep),\n+    .request_wen(request_wen), .request_ren(request_ren),\n+    .drain_barrier(drain_barrier), .dmem_ready(dmem_ready),\n+    .dmem_addr(dmem_addr), .dmem_wdata(dmem_wdata),\n+    .dmem_wen(dmem_wen), .dmem_ren(dmem_ren),\n+    .completion_wait(completion_wait)\n+  );\n+\n   // ── MEM/WB register ───────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   mem_wb_t reg_q;\n \n@@ -143,8 +158,8 @@ module mem_stage (\n       reg_q.mem_addr   <= aligned_addr;\n       reg_q.mem_rdata  <= dmem_rdata;\n       reg_q.mem_wdata  <= wdata_rep;\n-      reg_q.mem_wmask  <= (in.ctrl.mem_write && !mem_misalign) ? byte_mask : 4\'b0000;\n-      reg_q.mem_rmask  <= (in.ctrl.mem_read  && !mem_misalign) ? byte_mask : 4\'b0000;\n+      reg_q.mem_wmask  <= request_wen;\n+      reg_q.mem_rmask  <= request_ren ? byte_mask : 4\'b0000;\n       reg_q.ctrl       <= ctrl_with_trap;\n       reg_q.instr      <= in.instr;\n       reg_q.valid      <= in.valid;\n\n```\n\n## Your job\n\nAppend at most ONE bullet to cores/bench/LESSONS.md, in this format:\n\n  - YYYY-MM-DD <id> (<outcome>, <delta_pct>): <one-sentence lesson>\n\nA useful lesson:\n  - Captures *why* the outcome happened, not just *what* happened.\n  - Generalizes to future iterations (a pattern, gotcha, or design constraint),\n    not a one-off bug.\n  - Names specific RTL constructs / hazards / CoreMark behaviors when relevant.\n\nExamples of GOOD lessons (do not copy verbatim — write your own):\n  - "Dual-issue without a 2W register file creates WAW hazards riscv-formal\'s\n    \'reg\' check catches; reg file must expose two write ports."\n  - "64-entry BHT regressed CoreMark; the always-not-taken default was already\n    well-tuned to this benchmark\'s branch profile."\n  - "Adding a fetch buffer gained Fmax (+12 MHz) but blew the LUT budget by\n    30% on Tang Nano 20K — fetch-side area is the binding constraint."\n\nSKIP (write nothing — do not modify any file) if:\n  - The cause of the outcome is unclear from the available info.\n  - The bug was a trivial typo / missing default arm with no general lesson.\n  - This iteration plainly duplicates an already-recorded lesson.\n\nIf cores/bench/LESSONS.md does not exist, create it with the single bullet.\nIf it exists, APPEND to its end (preserve all prior content).\n\nDo NOT modify ANY other file. Do NOT print the bullet — write it to the file.\nDo NOT add headers, sections, prose, or anything other than the bullet line.\n']' returned non-zero exit status 1.
